.he "sed (1)                      8/16/26                      sed (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
sed
- stream editor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
sed [ -n ] [ -g ] [ -e script ] [ -f sfile ] [ file ] ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Sed copies the named files, or the standard input, to the standard
output, edited by a script of commands.  The -f option takes the
script from a file and the -e option from its argument; the two may be
mixed, and a single -e with no -f may be left off.  The -n option
turns off the default output.
.sp
A script is a list of commands, one to a line:
.sp
.nf
     [address [, address]] function [arguments]
.fi
.sp
In normal operation sed copies a line of input into the pattern space,
applies in order every command whose address selects it, and at the
end of the script copies the pattern space to the standard output,
unless -n was given, and starts again.
.sp
An address is a decimal line number counted across all the files, a $
for the last line of input, or /regular expression/ in the style of
ed.  The escape \n matches a newline embedded in the pattern space.
.sp
A command with no address selects every line; one address selects the
lines that match it; two select the range from the first line matching
the first through the next line matching the second.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-n	Do not print the pattern space at the end of each cycle.

.ti -10
-e script	Take the next argument as a command; several -e and -f options pile up.

.ti -10
-f sfile	Take the script from sfile.

.ti -10
-g	Make every s command global, as if each carried the g flag.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
The number in parentheses is the most addresses the command may take.
An argument written text is one or more lines, all but the last ending
in \ to hide the newline.
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
a\ text	Append.  Put text on the output before reading the next line.  (1)

.ti -10
b label	Branch to the : command bearing label; with no label, to the end of the script.  (2)

.ti -10
c\ text	Change.  Delete the pattern space, put text on the output, and start the next cycle.  (2)

.ti -10
d	Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle.  (2)

.ti -10
D	Delete the part of the pattern space up to the first newline and start the next cycle.  (2)

.ti -10
g	Replace the pattern space with the hold space.  (2)

.ti -10
G	Append the hold space to the pattern space.  (2)

.ti -10
h	Replace the hold space with the pattern space.  (2)

.ti -10
H	Append the pattern space to the hold space.  (2)

.ti -10
i\ text	Insert.  Put text on the output.  (1)

.ti -10
l	List.  Print the pattern space with non-graphic characters shown as escapes.  (2)

.ti -10
n	Print the pattern space and read the next line into it.  (2)

.ti -10
N	Append the next line to the pattern space with a newline between.  (2)

.ti -10
p	Print the pattern space.  (2)

.ti -10
P	Print the part of the pattern space up to the first newline.  (2)

.ti -10
q	Quit.  Print the pattern space and stop.  (1)

.ti -10
r rfile	Read rfile and put its contents on the output before the next line is read.  (1)

.ti -10
s/regular expression/replacement/flags	Substitute the replacement for the regular expression; any character may stand for /.  The flags are g to replace every non-overlapping instance, p to print the pattern space if a replacement was made, P to print just the first line of it, and w wfile to append it to wfile.  (2)

.ti -10
t label	Branch to the : command bearing label if a substitution has been made since the last line read or t.  (2)

.ti -10
w wfile	Append the pattern space to wfile.  (2)

.ti -10
x	Exchange the pattern space and the hold space.  (2)

.ti -10
y/string1/string2/	Replace each character of string1 with the one under it in string2, which must be the same length.  (2)

.ti -10
! function	Apply function only to the lines not selected by the address.  (2)

.ti -10
: label	Do nothing; a place for b and t to branch to.  (0)

.ti -10
=	Print the current line number.  (1)

.ti -10
{	Group; apply the commands through the matching } only when the pattern space is selected.  (2)

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The -g option, the l command and the P flag of s are in this sed but
not in the manual it came from, and the r command here takes one
address where the manual said two.
.sp
The buffers are smaller than the original's to fit one 64k image: the
longest line is 2000 characters, a script holds 128 commands and 128
line-number addresses, and the compiled commands share 5000 bytes.  A
script that overruns any of them says so and exits, and at most nine
w files can be named.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ed (1), grep (1)
